#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#: docs/guides/ci-cd.md:1
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Integrate the scanner into CI so the SBOM refreshes on every build and a "
"Critical-vulnerability gate can fail the pipeline."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #
#: docs/guides/ci-cd.md:5
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Use in CI/CD"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/ci-cd.md:8
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"An SBOM is a point-in-time snapshot of dependencies, so it must be "
"regenerated whenever dependencies change to stay in sync with the code. In "
"CI it refreshes on every build and release, attaches to release artifacts, "
"and becomes the basis for a vulnerability policy gate."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/ci-cd.md:10
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"> **Important**: the scanner is report-only — it reports vulnerabilities but "
"always exits successfully. To fail a build on Critical findings, add a step "
"that inspects the generated `*_security.json` (gate example below).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/ci-cd.md:12
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"To reduce load, split depth by trigger: on PRs generate the SBOM quickly "
"(`--generate-only --no-report`); on `main` and releases generate everything "
"(`--all --generate-only`) and apply the gate."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/ci-cd.md:16
#, markdown-text
msgid "The `ubuntu-latest` runner ships with `jq`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (yaml)
#: docs/guides/ci-cd.md:17
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"name: SBOM\n"
"\n"
"on:\n"
"  pull_request:\n"
"  push:\n"
"    branches: [main]\n"
"  release:\n"
"    types: [published]\n"
"\n"
"jobs:\n"
"  # PR: lightweight SBOM only (no report)\n"
"  sbom-pr:\n"
"    if: github.event_name == 'pull_request'\n"
"    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n"
"    steps:\n"
"      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n"
"      - run: docker pull ghcr.io/sktelecom/bomlens:latest\n"
"      - name: Generate SBOM (lightweight)\n"
"        run: |\n"
"          ./scripts/scan-sbom.sh \\\n"
"            --project \"${{ github.event.repository.name }}\" \\\n"
"            --version \"${{ github.sha }}\" \\\n"
"            --generate-only --no-report\n"
"      - uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4\n"
"        with:\n"
"          name: sbom-pr\n"
"          path: \"*_bom.json\"\n"
"\n"
"  # main/release: full generation + vulnerability gate\n"
"  sbom-full:\n"
"    if: github.event_name != 'pull_request'\n"
"    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n"
"    steps:\n"
"      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n"
"      - run: docker pull ghcr.io/sktelecom/bomlens:latest\n"
"      - name: Generate SBOM + reports\n"
"        run: |\n"
"          ./scripts/scan-sbom.sh \\\n"
"            --project \"${{ github.event.repository.name }}\" \\\n"
"            --version \"${{ github.sha }}\" \\\n"
"            --all --generate-only\n"
"\n"
"      # The scanner is report-only and always succeeds. Fail the build here "
"if Critical exists.\n"
"      - name: Fail on Critical vulnerabilities\n"
"        run: |\n"
"          CRIT=$(jq '[.Results[]?.Vulnerabilities[]? | "
"select(.Severity==\"CRITICAL\")] | length' *_security.json)\n"
"          echo \"Critical vulnerabilities: $CRIT\"\n"
"          if [ \"$CRIT\" -gt 0 ]; then\n"
"            echo \"::error::$CRIT critical vulnerability(ies) found\"\n"
"            exit 1\n"
"          fi\n"
"\n"
"      - uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4\n"
"        if: always()   # keep reports even when the gate fails\n"
"        with:\n"
"          name: sbom\n"
"          path: |\n"
"            *_bom.json\n"
"            *_security.*\n"
"            *_risk-report.*\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: docs/guides/ci-cd.md:80
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "GitLab CI"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/ci-cd.md:83
#, markdown-text
msgid "The `docker:latest` image has no `jq`, so install it before the gate."
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (yaml)
#: docs/guides/ci-cd.md:84
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"generate-sbom:\n"
"  stage: security\n"
"  image: docker:latest\n"
"  services:\n"
"    - docker:dind\n"
"  before_script:\n"
"    - apk add --no-cache jq\n"
"  script:\n"
"    - docker pull ghcr.io/sktelecom/bomlens:latest\n"
"    - ./scripts/scan-sbom.sh\n"
"        --project \"$CI_PROJECT_NAME\"\n"
"        --version \"$CI_COMMIT_SHA\"\n"
"        --all --generate-only\n"
"    # Use the report-only scanner as a build gate: fail if Critical exists\n"
"    - |\n"
"      CRIT=$(jq '[.Results[]?.Vulnerabilities[]? | "
"select(.Severity==\"CRITICAL\")] | length' *_security.json)\n"
"      [ \"$CRIT\" -eq 0 ] || { echo \"$CRIT critical vulnerability(ies) "
"found\"; exit 1; }\n"
"  artifacts:\n"
"    when: always\n"
"    paths:\n"
"      - \"*_bom.json\"\n"
"      - \"*_security.*\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:1
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Unpack network-device firmware binaries (.bin, squashfs, and more) to "
"identify components and check the SBOM, licenses, and vulnerabilities with "
"BomLens firmware analysis."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:5
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Firmware analysis guide"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:8
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"How to identify components and check the SBOM, licenses, and vulnerabilities "
"in a network-device firmware binary (`.bin`, `.img`, squashfs, and so "
"on). Use it when you only have the binary and no source, such as firmware "
"received from a supplier."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:12
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Firmware is a single file that packs and seals an operating system and "
"dozens of libraries. Feeding a firmware file straight into a normal scan "
"detects almost nothing and yields an empty SBOM. Firmware analysis first "
"unpacks the contents, then identifies components."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:31
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Unpack the firmware (unblob, falling back to `unsquashfs`, `7z`, then "
"`binwalk`) to extract the rootfs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:31
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Use `syft` to identify components installed by a package manager (opkg, "
"dpkg, apk, rpm).  An image can carry more than one filesystem, and a tree "
"sitting beside the rootfs rather than inside it is cataloged too. Three "
"kinds are recognized, each by the record of what is installed in it rather "
"than by its directory name: a container image store, identified by the image "
"index Docker keeps (the usual layout on a switch OS, where most of what runs "
"is in a container); a second filesystem carrying its own package database; "
"and an interpreter's installed library set, such as a Python "
"`site-packages`. Set `FW_EXTRA_ROOTS=false` to read only the "
"rootfs. Components from a container store record the image they belong to in "
"`bomlens:container:image`, and the images themselves appear as `container` "
"components. A package installed by `dpkg` inside a container is identified "
"by the distribution the container runs, read from the `os-release` in its "
"own layers, so that the vulnerability step has something to match it "
"against; those carry `bomlens:purlSource`. An SPDX export keeps this: the "
"images and the distribution are packages there, and the membership is a "
"`CONTAINS` relationship."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:31
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Use `cve-bin-tool` to find the versions and vulnerabilities of stripped "
"static binaries (busybox, openssl, dropbear, and so on)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '4. '
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:31
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Merge the two results into one SBOM, then run the same post-processing as a "
"normal scan (licenses, CVEs, signing)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:32
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Preparing the firmware image"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:35
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Firmware analysis needs a separate image that bundles the unpacking and "
"binary-identification tools (unblob, cve-bin-tool, and so on). These are "
"GPL-family tools, so they are kept out of the lightweight base image and "
"split into an opt-in firmware image."
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:36
#, no-wrap
msgid "docker pull ghcr.io/sktelecom/bomlens-firmware:latest\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:41
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"This image is the default, so adding `--firmware` pulls it without any extra "
"setting. To use a different tag, set the environment variable "
"`SBOM_FIRMWARE_IMAGE`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:43
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"In the web UI and the desktop app you do not have to run that command: the "
"firmware input offers a Download now button with the download size, and "
"shows how many layers are done while it runs. Without it the image is still "
"pulled on the first firmware scan, so the only difference is whether the "
"wait happens before the scan or inside it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:47
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Firmware analysis needs the firmware image (above), whether you use the web "
"UI or the CLI."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:51
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Launch the web UI as usual — you do not need to point it at this image "
"yourself. As long as the Docker engine is running, the base UI image "
"launches this firmware image as a sibling container over the Docker socket, "
"and the firmware upload tile appears automatically."
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:52
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"./scripts/scan-sbom.sh --ui\n"
"#   Windows: just double-click sbom-ui.bat\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:58
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Set `SBOM_FIRMWARE_IMAGE` only if you want a different tag or an internal "
"mirror registry. It takes effect only as a real environment variable — the "
"settings file `bomlens.settings.txt` that a double-clicked `.bat` reads does "
"not support this key."
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:59
#, no-wrap
msgid "SBOM_FIRMWARE_IMAGE=<your-mirror>:<tag> ./scripts/scan-sbom.sh --ui\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:64
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Enter a project name and version, choose the firmware upload tile, upload "
"the file, and run. While the CVE database downloads on a first online run, "
"the UI shows a download progress bar."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:68
#, markdown-text
msgid "Pass the firmware file you received to `--target` and add `--firmware`:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:69
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"./scripts/scan-sbom.sh --project device-fw --version 1.0.0 \\\n"
"  --target \"./device.bin\" --firmware \\\n"
"  --all --generate-only\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:77
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Recognized extensions (`.bin`, `.img`, `.squashfs`, `.ubi`, `.ubifs`, "
"`.trx`, `.chk`, `.fw`, `.rom`) are auto-detected even without `--firmware`, "
"but being explicit is recommended."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:77
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"The outputs are the same three as a normal scan: the notice (`_NOTICE`), the "
"SBOM (`_bom.json`), and the risk report (`_risk-report`)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:78
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "CVE matching, online and offline"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:81
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"CVE matching for static binaries uses cve-bin-tool with its own "
"vulnerability database. The firmware image ships in a hybrid arrangement, so "
"the same image works both air-gapped and online."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:85
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"When the image is built with the database bundled, firmware scans match CVEs "
"offline at scan time. This is the fast, air-gap-friendly path."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:85
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"When the bundled database is absent but the network is reachable, "
"cve-bin-tool downloads the database from NVD at runtime. The first run is "
"slow; the web UI shows a download progress bar while it runs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:85
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"When neither a bundled database nor a network is available, the scan "
"degrades to component-only identification (no CVEs) and logs the reason "
"rather than silently dropping the CVE stage."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:87
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"`CVE_BIN_TOOL_MODE` selects the behavior: `auto` (default; bundled database "
"first, otherwise download if online), `offline`, `online`, or "
"`components-only`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:89
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"The database is aggregated from several sources (NVD, PURL2CPE, and others), "
"not from NVD alone. cve-bin-tool prints the notice \"This product uses the "
"NVD API but is not endorsed or certified by the NVD.\""
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:91
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"OSV (Open Source Vulnerabilities) advisories are not bundled, to keep the "
"redistributed image free of share-alike data. The web UI offers an opt-in "
"toggle, \"Include OSV advisories,\" that fetches OSV from osv.dev for that "
"single scan, so the data is downloaded directly on your machine rather than "
"shipped in the image."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:92
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "License note"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:95
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"The firmware image contains GPL tools (cve-bin-tool, sasquatch, and some "
"extractors that unblob depends on). The shell scripts only invoke them as "
"separate processes, so copyleft does not propagate into our code, but "
"redistributing GPL binaries in an image carries the obligation to include "
"the license texts and offer the source. For the full inventory, see [Bundled "
"tool "
"licenses](https://github.com/sktelecom/bomlens/blob/main/THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md). "
"The GPL analysis tools live only in this firmware image. Every image "
"including the base one is Debian-based, though, so all of them also carry "
"GPL system packages; source directions for those are in the same document."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:102
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"The open-source tool stack detects roughly 60–85% of components, and the "
"result depends heavily on the firmware type, how aggressively it is "
"stripped, and whether unpacking succeeds."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:102
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Without function-level binary fingerprinting, stripped or inlined components "
"and binaries with version strings removed are missed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:102
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Statically linked libraries, vendor-modified squashfs, encrypted or signed "
"firmware, and renamed libraries go undetected or are misidentified."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:102
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"The resulting SBOM is a best-effort estimate, so do not use it as the sole "
"basis for legal license compliance."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/firmware.md:105
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"> **Related**: [Getting started](../start/first-scan.md) | [Scenarios "
"guide](../guides/by-input.md) | [CLI reference](../reference/cli.md) | "
"[Notice and security guide](../guides/reports.md)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:1
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Identify open source copied (vendored) into C/C++ embedded source that has "
"no package manager, so a normal BomLens scan that finds almost nothing turns "
"into a real component list with versions and CVEs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:5
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Identify bundled open source (C/C++)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:8
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Use this when you scan a C/C++ embedded source tree and BomLens finds almost "
"nothing."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:9
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When you need it"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:12
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"A normal scan reads a package manager (npm, Maven, pip, Go, Conan, and so "
"on) to learn what open source a project uses. C/C++ embedded firmware "
"usually has no package manager: the open source is copied straight into the "
"source tree (this is called *vendored* source — for example a copy of "
"openssl, zlib, or liblfds under `third_party/`). cdxgen cannot name those "
"files, so the SBOM comes back almost empty, with each file listed as an "
"unidentified `pkg:generic` entry."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:14
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"When that happens, BomLens prints a one-line hint suggesting this option, "
"and the web UI shows the same hint after the scan. You do not need to "
"recognize the situation yourself."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:16
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"![Result banner suggesting identify-vendored for a sparse C/C++ "
"scan](../images/web-ui-vendored-banner-en.png)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:18
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"`--identify-vendored` matches the file fingerprints of your sources against "
"the public OSSKB knowledge base and records each match as a real component "
"(name, version, PURL), so the copied-in open source shows up in the SBOM — "
"and, where the library has known CVEs, in the security report."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:19
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What is sent"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:22
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Only file **fingerprints** (hashes) are sent to the OSSKB service. Your "
"source code never leaves the machine. The supplier can run this in their own "
"environment before any contract."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:23
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "On a package-managed project"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:26
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"This option is for source with no package manager. If your project uses npm, "
"Maven, pip, Go, and so on, the normal scan already resolves your "
"dependencies and you do not need it. If you turn it on anyway, BomLens "
"reconciles the results: dependency and build directories (`node_modules`, "
"`vendor`, `dist`, and the like) are skipped, and any match whose name a "
"package-manager component already carries is dropped in favor of that "
"authoritative identity. So enabling it on a managed project does not "
"duplicate known dependencies or inflate the vulnerability count — at most it "
"adds genuinely copied-in source the package manager could not see."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:28
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Matches are recorded read-only, tagged with their source and "
"confidence. BomLens does not provide an accept/reject audit workflow; if you "
"need to confirm or triage matches, upload the SBOM to a vulnerability "
"management system (Dependency-Track, TRUSCA, etc.) and do it there."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:29 docs/guides/reports.md:37
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Prerequisites"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:32
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"The published `bomlens` image (v1.4.0 and later) already includes the "
"SCANOSS client, so no extra setup is needed. If you build the image yourself "
"with a minimal configuration, add the build arg:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:33
#, no-wrap
msgid "docker build --build-arg SBOM_SCANOSS=true -t bomlens ./docker\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:37
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Run it"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:39
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"scan-sbom.sh --project trelay --version 26.4.0 --target ./src \\\n"
"  --identify-vendored --all --generate-only\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:45
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"In the web UI or desktop app, open **Advanced** and turn on **File-level "
"identification (SCANOSS)** — the on-screen label differs from this guide's "
"title, but it is the same feature. The option appears only for a source scan "
"(current directory, git URL, or ZIP upload) when the image supports it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:47
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"If you are on Windows and new to the command line, follow the desktop-app "
"steps in [Quick start without the CLI](../start/no-cli.md) first."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:49
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"![The Advanced section with the File-level identification (SCANOSS) "
"toggle](../images/web-ui-identify-vendored-en.png)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:50
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What you get"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:55
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Copied-in open source appears in the SBOM as named components with versions, "
"each tagged `vendored` (a `bomlens:layer=vendored` property)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:55
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Components that map to a known product get a CPE, so the Trivy security "
"report lists their CVEs. For example a vendored `openssl 1.1.1w` shows up "
"with its advisories."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:55
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Niche libraries with no entry in the vulnerability databases (for example "
"`liblfds`, `libaes`, `djbdns`) are still identified by name and version; "
"they simply have no CVEs to report, which is a limit of the public data, not "
"of the scan."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:57
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Only full-file matches become components. Partial (snippet) matches are "
"noisy and are left out, so the report stays clean."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:59
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"![Components table with vendored components tagged and their match "
"confidence](../images/web-ui-vendored-badge-en.png)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:60
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Endpoint and limits"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:63
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"The default endpoint is the free OSSKB API, which is rate-limited and "
"intended for identification only. From the CLI, you can point at a SCANOSS "
"commercial or self-hosted endpoint with environment variables for "
"high-volume or air-gapped use:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:64
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"SCANOSS_API_URL=https://your-scanoss-endpoint \\\n"
"SCANOSS_API_KEY=your-key \\\n"
"scan-sbom.sh --project trelay --version 26.4.0 --target ./src "
"--identify-vendored --all --generate-only\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:71
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"In the web UI and desktop app, you can supply only the token from the "
"screen. If you hit the free OSSKB rate limit, turn on **File-level "
"identification (SCANOSS)** and paste a token from scanoss.com into the field "
"that appears below the toggle, then run again. The token is used once for "
"that scan and is never stored or logged."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:73
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"The endpoint URL (`SCANOSS_API_URL`) and the reporting threshold "
"(`SCANOSS_MIN_FILES`) are set through CLI or container environment variables "
"only; neither the web UI nor the desktop app exposes a field for them. In "
"particular, the desktop app does not forward `SCANOSS_API_URL` into the "
"container, so a commercial or self-hosted endpoint cannot be used from the "
"desktop app today. If you need one, run from the CLI or `sbom-ui.bat` with "
"the variable set."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:75
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Version precision is approximate. A file match reports the release where "
"that file content first appeared, so different files of the same library can "
"resolve to slightly different versions and a copied-in library may be "
"reported a point release off. Treat the version (and any CVEs derived from "
"it) as a starting point for review, not a final verdict."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:77
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Attribution can also point at the wrong project. A file that many projects "
"copy (for example zlib's `deflate.c`) may match a downstream project that "
"vendored it rather than the canonical upstream. To cut that noise, BomLens "
"reports a library only when at least two files agree on it (configurable "
"with `SCANOSS_MIN_FILES`; set `1` to keep every match) and resolves the "
"version and PURL from the consensus across those files, so scattered one-off "
"fork matches are dropped and a library split across forks collapses to a "
"single component. This helps but does not fully fix it — a real copy can "
"still be reported under another name, and its CVEs missed. It is a ranking "
"and coverage limit of the knowledge base, more pronounced on the free OSSKB; "
"for higher-fidelity attribution, point `SCANOSS_API_URL` at a SCANOSS "
"commercial or self-hosted endpoint."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:79
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Separately, scanning source that is itself published in a public repository "
"will match that repository (your own first-party files can match your own "
"public project) — this does not occur for the intended case of private "
"supplier source."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: docs/guides/identify-vendored.md:80
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Results are a best-effort estimate that benefits from human review. See the "
"OSSKB terms and license notes in "
"[THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md](https://github.com/sktelecom/bomlens/blob/main/THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
